Task 1: Access the Jaeger UI
Subtask 1.1: Verify Jaeger Installation
First, let's confirm that Jaeger is properly installed and running in your cluster.

Open your terminal and check the Jaeger pods:
kubectl get pods -n istio-system | grep jaeger
Expected output should show Jaeger pods in Running status:

jaeger-5d44bc5c5d-89s2x    1/1     Running   0          2m
Verify Jaeger service is available:
kubectl get svc -n istio-system | grep jaeger
You should see output similar to:

jaeger-query     ClusterIP   10.96.123.45   <none>        16686/TCP   2m
Subtask 1.2: Access Jaeger UI
Create a port-forward to access Jaeger UI from your local machine:
kubectl port-forward -n istio-system svc/jaeger-query 16686:16686 &
Open your web browser and navigate to:
http://localhost:16686
You should see the Jaeger UI homepage with the following sections:
Service dropdown: Lists all services being traced
Operation dropdown: Shows operations for selected service
Tags section: For filtering traces
Lookback period: Time range for trace search
Subtask 1.3: Explore Jaeger UI Components
Take a moment to familiarize yourself with the Jaeger interface:

Search tab: Main interface for finding and filtering traces
Compare tab: Compare multiple traces side by side
System Architecture tab: Visual representation of service dependencies
Monitor tab: Real-time monitoring dashboard
Task 2: Track Requests Through Microservices
Subtask 2.1: Deploy Sample Application
Deploy the Bookinfo sample application to generate trace data:
kubectl apply -f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/istio/istio/release-1.20/samples/bookinfo/platform/kube/bookinfo.yaml
Enable Istio sidecar injection for the default namespace:
kubectl label namespace default istio-injection=enabled
Verify all pods are running with Istio sidecars:
kubectl get pods
You should see 2/2 containers for each pod (application + Istio proxy).

Create an Istio Gateway and VirtualService:
kubectl apply -f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/istio/istio/release-1.20/samples/bookinfo/networking/bookinfo-gateway.yaml
Subtask 2.2: Generate Traffic for Tracing
Get the ingress gateway URL:
export INGRESS_HOST=$(kubectl get po -l istio=ingressgateway -n istio-system -o jsonpath='{.items[0].status.hostIP}')
export INGRESS_PORT=$(kubectl -n istio-system get service istio-ingressgateway -o jsonpath='{.spec.ports[?(@.name=="http2")].nodePort}')
export GATEWAY_URL=$INGRESS_HOST:$INGRESS_PORT
Generate multiple requests to create trace data:
for i in {1..20}; do
  curl -s "http://$GATEWAY_URL/productpage" > /dev/null
  echo "Request $i sent"
  sleep 1
done
Generate requests with different user agents to create varied traces:
curl -H "User-Agent: Mobile-App" "http://$GATEWAY_URL/productpage"
curl -H "User-Agent: Web-Browser" "http://$GATEWAY_URL/productpage"
curl -H "User-Agent: API-Client" "http://$GATEWAY_URL/productpage"
Subtask 2.3: View Traces in Jaeger UI
Return to the Jaeger UI at http://localhost:16686

In the Service dropdown, select productpage.default

Click Find Traces to see all traces for the productpage service

You should see a list of traces with the following information:

Trace ID: Unique identifier for each request
Duration: Total time for the request
Services: Number of services involved
Spans: Number of operations in the trace
Click on one of the traces to view detailed information

Subtask 2.4: Analyze Trace Details
When you click on a trace, you'll see a detailed timeline view showing:

Service calls: Each service involved in the request
Span duration: Time spent in each service
Parent-child relationships: How services call each other
Examine the trace structure for a typical Bookinfo request:

productpage → details (service call)
productpage → reviews (service call)
reviews → ratings (conditional service call)
Click on individual spans to see:

Operation name: Specific operation performed
Tags: Metadata about the operation
Logs: Any log messages associated with the span
Process information: Details about the service instance
Task 3: Identify Latency and Errors in Request Flow
Subtask 3.1: Introduce Artificial Delays
Create a delay in the ratings service to simulate latency issues:
kubectl apply -f - <<EOF
apiVersion: networking.istio.io/v1alpha3
kind: VirtualService
metadata:
  name: ratings
spec:
  hosts:
  - ratings
  http:
  - fault:
      delay:
        percentage:
          value: 50.0
        fixedDelay: 2s
    route:
    - destination:
        host: ratings
        subset: v1
EOF
Create the required DestinationRule:
kubectl apply -f - <<EOF
apiVersion: networking.istio.io/v1alpha3
kind: DestinationRule
metadata:
  name: ratings
spec:
  host: ratings
  subsets:
  - name: v1
    labels:
      version: v1
EOF
Subtask 3.2: Generate Traffic with Delays
Generate new requests to see the delay effect:
for i in {1..10}; do
  curl -s "http://$GATEWAY_URL/productpage" > /dev/null
  echo "Request $i with potential delay sent"
  sleep 1
done
Subtask 3.3: Analyze Latency in Jaeger
Return to Jaeger UI and search for new traces

Look for traces with longer durations (should be around 2+ seconds for affected requests)

Click on a slow trace and observe:

The ratings service span shows increased duration
The delay is clearly visible in the timeline
Parent spans (reviews, productpage) also show increased total time
Compare fast and slow traces by:

Opening multiple browser tabs with different traces
Using the Compare feature in Jaeger
Noting the difference in span durations
Subtask 3.4: Introduce HTTP Errors
Create an HTTP abort fault for the ratings service:
kubectl apply -f - <<EOF
apiVersion: networking.istio.io/v1alpha3
kind: VirtualService
metadata:
  name: ratings
spec:
  hosts:
  - ratings
  http:
  - fault:
      abort:
        percentage:
          value: 30.0
        httpStatus: 500
    route:
    - destination:
        host: ratings
        subset: v1
EOF
Generate traffic to trigger errors:
for i in {1..15}; do
  curl -s "http://$GATEWAY_URL/productpage" > /dev/null
  echo "Request $i with potential error sent"
  sleep 1
done
Subtask 3.5: Identify Errors in Traces
In Jaeger UI, look for traces with error indicators:

Red-colored spans indicate errors
Error tags in span details
HTTP status codes other than 2xx
Click on an error trace and examine:

Tags section: Look for http.status_code: 500
Error tag: error: true
Logs section: Error messages and stack traces
Use Jaeger's filtering capabilities:

Add tag filter: error=true
Filter by minimum duration for slow requests
Search by specific operation names
